    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1018">

                <pb facs="1375" n="1251" sameAs="frus:frus1923v02/pg1251"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.602 Ot 81/437: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The High Commissioner at Constantinople</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bristol</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-12-21T15:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 21,
                                1923—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 21—3
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>335. The Turkish Minister of Public Works on December 18 notified Soubhi Bey
                    through a notary that the Chester concession had been annulled. The company’s
                    representative on the same day <pb facs="1376" n="1252" xml:id="pg1252"/>
                    ordered that work at Samsoun be discontinued. There is, however, reason to think
                    that should Clayton-Kennedy reach Angora reasonably soon with proof that he had
                    substantial financial support the contract would be revived.<anchor n="80"
                        xml:id="d1018-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bristol</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1018-f1" n="80" xml:id="d1018-f1-content">
                <p>The High Commissioner at Constantinople telegraphed Mar. 10, 1925, that there was
                    no reason to believe that the Turkish Government had changed its attitude toward
                    the Chester concession and that practically the “concession as negotiated in
                    1923 is dead”. (File no. 867.602 Ot 81/462.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
